## The red items of the updated Appendix C "Semantics" and Section 3.1

1. **"for a measurement \ph{the current value}"** (also Section 3.1). *Matches; remove the red.*
   - A measurement criterion counts exactly one value, the patient's current value.
   - There is no "most recent" rule. `Criterion.evaluate` raises an error on zero or two counted
     values, so the generator cannot produce such a state.
   - Earlier values are separate mentions with time "past", and the criterion does not count them.
     {{semantics.all_test_and_dev.numeric_inputs_with_older_value}} measured inputs carry one. An
     earlier value is dated with a year, or is superseded. In the test templates a superseded value
     reads "Earlier this week, X was V; a newer reading supersedes it." or "Last month, X was V; the
     newest measurement replaces it.". So write "an older value (dated with a year, or superseded)".
   - No measurement criterion counts past values
     ({{library_semantics.numeric_criteria_counting_past}} of {{library_semantics.numeric_criteria}}).
   - In the labelled test and development cases, all but missing twins:
     {{semantics.all_test_and_dev.numeric_inputs_one_counted_value}} of
     {{semantics.all_test_and_dev.numeric_inputs}} measured inputs have exactly one current value.
     Missing twins drop the decisive measurement on purpose.
